{"type":"system","subtype":"background_tasks_changed","tasks":[{"task_id":"bh6u5ikpy","task_type":"local_bash","description":"cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop; D=iter_2/candidate_5; M=models\npython3 - <<'EOF'\nsrc = open(\"models/length_dependent_ideal_switching.py\").read()\ndoc_old = src[:src.index('\"\"\"', 3)+3]\ndoc_new = '''\"\"\"Length-dependent ideal switching with complement-symmetry aversion.\n\nRefinement of `length_dependent_ideal_switching`: people still hold a personal\nideal switching rate that shifts with sequence length by a shared amount (law of\nsmall numbers), with lenient over-alternation, personal weights on imbalance and\nthe longest run, shared weights on the final run, mirror symmetry, run-length\nvariety and the most lopsided stretch, and a personal lapse rate. The one change\nis grafting in the incumbent's complement-symmetry aversion: a sequence of four\nor more flips that equals its own reverse with heads and tails swapped looks\ndeliberately constructed, and so less random, by a shared weight.\n\"\"\"'''\nsrc = src.replace(doc_old, doc_new)\nsrc = src.replace('''    a = sequence_a.strip().upper()''', '''    def complement_symmetric(seq):\n        if len(seq) < 4:\n            return 0.0\n        flipped = \"\".join(\"T\" if c == \"H\" else \"H\" for c in seq[::-1])\n        return 1.0 if seq == flipped else 0.0\n\n    a = sequence_a.strip().upper()''')\nsrc = src.replace('''        \"length_c\": (len(a) - 5.0) / 3.0,\n''', '''        \"antisym_a\": complement_symmetric(a),\n        \"antisym_b\": complement_symmetric(b),\n        \"length_c\": (len(a) - 5.0) / 3.0,\n''')\nsrc = src.replace('''    length_c = pm.Data(\"length_c\"''', '''    antisym_a = pm.Data(\"antisym_a\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\"))\n    antisym_b = pm.Data(\"antisym_b\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\"))\n    length_c = pm.Data(\"length_c\"''')\nsrc = src.replace('''    # Shared fraction of the ideal-rate penalty''', '''    # Shared penalty on complement symmetry (positive: antisymmetric sequences look designed).\n    chi = pm.Normal(\"chi\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.5)\n\n    # Shared fraction of the ideal-rate penalty''')\nsrc = src.replace(\"- eta * lopsided_a\\n\", \"- eta * lopsided_a - chi * antisym_a\\n\")\nsrc = src.replace(\"- eta * lopsided_b\\n\", \"- eta * lopsided_b - chi * antisym_b\\n\")\nassert src.count(\"antisym_a\") == 4 and src.count(\"chi *\") == 2, (src.count(\"antisym_a\"), src.count(\"chi *\"))\nopen(\"iter_2/candidate_5/candidate.py\", \"w\").write(src)\nEOF\necho length_switching_complement_symmetry > $D/model_name.txt\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md <<'EOF'\nRefinement of `length_dependent_ideal_switching` (rank 1): people still apply the law of small numbers to switching — the switching rate they consider ideal for a random coin shifts with how many flips they are looking at, by a shared amount on top of each person's own ideal — and still judge each sequence by their personal sensitivity to that ideal (lenient toward over-alternation), personal weights on heads/tails imbalance and the longest streak, shared weights on the final streak, mirror symmetry, run-length variety and the most lopsided stretch, with a personal guessing rate. The one change is grafting in the incumbent's complement-symmetry aversion: a sequence of four or more flips that equals its own reverse with heads and tails swapped (HHTT, HHHTHTTT, HTHTHTHT) looks deliberately constructed and so less random, by a shared weight — so the length-dependent ideal is estimated on top of the best current judgement, predicting that on short versus long pairs differing mainly in switching rate the preferred amount of alternation shifts with length even after antisymmetric sequences are penalised.\nEOF\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run1/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --cand